WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.930 --> 00:00:04.300
I'm sitting here with Mike,
one of the web developers at Udacity.

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:07.220
I heard you wrote some
pretty bad code recently.

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:09.080
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
[LAUGH] That was quite the experience.

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:12.775
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I actually remember, because
I gave you that bad code assignment.

00:00:12.775 --> 00:00:13.460
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] You did.

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:17.378
&gt;&gt; So, so basically in a nutshell,
what did I tell you to do?

00:00:17.378 --> 00:00:22.040
&gt;&gt; You, you came up with an app idea,
and you just told me, all right,

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:27.660
now forget all the things you know
to do right, and, and do it wrong.

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:30.250
And try to make it a rough experience.

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:31.940
&gt;&gt; So build a spaghetti code app.

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:33.620
&gt;&gt; Build a spaghetti code application.

00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:34.570
&gt;&gt; And how did you feel?

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:39.330
I was really excited because I knew
I didn't have to maintain this thing.

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:41.460
I was just going to crank
out an application for

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:43.850
you really quick, get it done in hour or

00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:48.690
two and and just get code on
the screen and feel super productive.

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:52.570
So I was, I was kind of excited to not
have to put whole lot of brain power to

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:53.880
it and just do it.

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:56.450
Yeah it's kind of old
habits in a way right.

00:00:56.450 --> 00:00:57.860
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah.
&gt;&gt; Because everyone kind of learns

00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:00.710
spaghetti code and then learns how
to organize their code later on.

00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:03.130
&gt;&gt; It reminded me of like ten years ago.

00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:05.055
Like the type of work I was doing.

00:01:05.055 --> 00:01:06.460
[LAUGH].

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:08.190
&gt;&gt; So you had this app.

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:09.700
You didn't have to maintain it.

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:10.725
You felt great about it.

00:01:10.725 --> 00:01:12.450
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.
&gt;&gt; How do you feel by the end?

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:13.644
&gt;&gt; Pretty bad.

00:01:13.644 --> 00:01:14.330
[LAUGH].

00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:15.320
&gt;&gt; Really, why?

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:19.440
&gt;&gt; Well it was a miserable
experience when it got to the end.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:23.260
There was a point that I actually
coded myself into a corner.

00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:23.980
&gt;&gt; Oh.
&gt;&gt; And

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:29.670
I had a problem that I knew, like I knew
myself was a simple problem to solve.

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:32.470
But, because of the way I
had built the application

00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:36.540
it was no longer a simple problem to
solve, it was actually really difficult.

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:40.180
And then I just felt bad because I,
I knew where this code was going.

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:40.880
&gt;&gt; I see.

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:43.510
Well, actually,
Nick Artman one of our other front end

00:01:43.510 --> 00:01:44.150
engineers-
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:46.870
&gt;&gt; Likens it to diving for
the finish line.

00:01:46.870 --> 00:01:47.990
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; And if you dive for

00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:51.310
the finish line too early,
well, you get scraped up.

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:52.810
Right?
And things seem really bad and

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:55.840
it takes a lot more effort to go
then and finish the, the race.

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:56.750
&gt;&gt; That's-.

00:01:56.750 --> 00:01:58.630
&gt;&gt; That's a very good metaphor about it.

00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:01.400
&gt;&gt; I basically told you to die
right out of the starting block.

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:02.270
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Right.

00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:03.963
Yeah.
I, I thought I was done with

00:02:03.963 --> 00:02:07.677
the application before I had ever
started it really, and so I was just

00:02:07.677 --> 00:02:11.453
[NOISE] trying to get stuff done and
if I would have taken my time to like,

00:02:11.453 --> 00:02:14.973
really plan it out ahead of time and,
and know what I was going to do

00:02:14.973 --> 00:02:19.424
throughout everything it would have
been a much more enjoyable experience.

00:02:19.424 --> 00:02:19.930
&gt;&gt; All right.

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:23.460
So if you want to blame someone for
this experience you can blame Mike.

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:25.040
&gt;&gt; You told me to do it.

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:27.510
&gt;&gt; If you want to blame someone for
this experience, you can blame me.

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:29.190
Bad advice.
[LAUGH] Awesome.

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:30.315
Well, thanks Mike for sitting with me.

00:02:30.315 --> 00:02:31.554
&gt;&gt; Uh-huh.

